{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means less visual clutter—not less comfort.** In a small flat, it’s about choosing a complete, useful set of furniture that leaves room to move.\n\n- **Purposeful pieces:** comfortable seating, a reachable table, lighting and enough storage—not an almost-empty room.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so doors, windows and everyday paths stay accessible.\n- **A restrained palette:** a few complementary colours and materials rather than many competing finishes.\n- **Mostly closed storage:** keep everyday necessities handy without displaying everything.\n- **Warmth through texture:** wood, woven fabrics and a rug can feel welcoming without looking busy.\n\nFor your combined living and dining room, I’d suggest two clearly defined zones with coordinated furniture, rather than lots of small pieces scattered throughout. Your existing warm off-white walls could be a good starting point.", "conversationId": "1e9f74b295b94bc8b4c76b094f8e958c"}
